{"command":"record_issue_mutation","content":{"intent_digest":"86238ac773496ecb795f5b72fd8e85c571293c865b2bd1fc77efdbfa4f918774","operation_digest":"1ff5d94e2682673692dee4d22a91f007198aeb0f6b62259a122ef4dc465eebc7","request":{"credential_names":["GITHUB_TOKEN"],"cutover_issue":null,"expected_head_sha":"9c18d682bf28f88db8b47279ac2ab5ab2e41db68","issue":970,"mutation":{"action":"issue_edit","body":"## Problem\n\n`AProvider` is a declarative, text-based provider configuration consumed by trusted provider runtime code. It is not executable code and must never become a provider-specific binary, script, plugin, command, or workflow authority.\n\nThe current defect is narrower: provider profiles and provider definitions can declare bounded inference parameters that the selected request path does not consistently consume or reject. The #900 six-resident Ollama qualification exposed the concrete gap: configuration recorded a 32K context and bounded generation settings while the Ollama HTTP request used the server default context unless the server was configured separately. `top_p`, output limits, deterministic seed, and related controls also lack one uniform effective-configuration contract.\n\n#514 established shared inference profiles, #876 established validated editable provider definitions and last-known-good reload, and #855 established provider-neutral lifecycle. #901 owns provider failure and recovery qualification. This issue is the bounded configuration-to-request correction.\n\n## Architecture boundary\n\nThe intended flow is:\n\n```text\nprovider profile or explicit AProvider text\n                 |\n                 v\n       parse, expand, and validate\n                 |\n                 v\n  normalized effective AProvider configuration\n                 |\n                 v\n trusted GeneralProvider runtime module\n                 |\n                 v\n built-in transport/auth/wire-format codec\n                 |\n                 v\n        provider service or local runtime\n```\n\n- `AProvider` contains data: provider identity, model, endpoint reference, credential reference, capabilities, transport selection, and bounded inference parameters.\n- `GeneralProvider` owns execution, dispatch, validation, redaction, observability, and selection of trusted built-in codecs.\n- Ollama, OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic, Bedrock, Vertex, and other protocol differences are built-in codecs or transport implementations behind `GeneralProvider`; they are not executable provider definitions.\n- An `AProvider` document cannot introduce code, executable paths, commands, scripts, dynamic libraries, arbitrary plugins, or lifecycle/workflow authority.\n\n## Required outcome\n\nDefine and implement one canonical effective inference-configuration boundary. A validated `AProvider` configuration must either reach the selected built-in transport/codec request with auditable effective values or fail before dispatch with an explicit unsupported-configuration classification. No declared value may be silently treated as effective when it was ignored.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Define the canonical text schema and normalized representation for `AProvider`, including provider/model identity, endpoint and credential references, capabilities, transport/codec selection, context window, output-token limit, temperature, top-p, deterministic seed, timeout, reasoning controls, and applicable local keep-alive/resource controls.\n- [ ] Provider profiles expand deterministically into the same normalized `AProvider` representation used by explicit provider configuration. Profiles do not create a separate execution path.\n- [ ] `GeneralProvider` is the single trusted execution boundary. Registered provider variants select only built-in transports, authentication strategies, and wire-format codecs.\n- [ ] Validation rejects executable paths, commands, scripts, dynamic libraries, arbitrary plugin references, embedded code, and workflow or lifecycle authority in `AProvider` data.\n- [ ] Every built-in codec declares which normalized controls it consumes. Any supplied but unsupported control fails before provider execution with a bounded actionable classification.\n- [ ] The Ollama HTTP codec forwards bounded context, output, temperature, top-p, seed/think, and keep-alive controls supported by the pinned Ollama API. Local CLI behavior remains an explicitly separate built-in transport.\n- [ ] Profile expansion, editable-definition reload, Runtime dispatch, and direct `GeneralProvider` invocation produce the same effective configuration for the same selected snapshot.\n- [ ] A redacted effective-configuration projection and request fingerprint bind the values actually sent without retaining prompts, credential values, private endpoint data, or provider response content.\n- [ ] Focused wire-capture tests prove every supported field reaches the provider request. Negative tests prove invalid, conflicting, excessive, executable, and unsupported settings fail before any call.\n- [ ] Existing profiles and provider definitions receive an explicit compatibility or migration disposition. Defaults remain truthful and no previously advertised field is claimed effective without execution proof.\n- [ ] Operator documentation distinguishes `AProvider` data, `GeneralProvider` runtime behavior, built-in provider codecs, server-controlled settings, and unsupported controls.\n- [ ] Exact-head independent review and required CI pass before merge.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Declarative `AProvider` schema and normalized inference-configuration contract.\n- Provider-profile expansion into `AProvider`.\n- `GeneralProvider` validation, dispatch, redacted projection, and request fingerprinting.\n- Built-in adapter/codec request construction, beginning with Ollama HTTP as the proving path.\n- Focused provider tests and provider operator documentation.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Executable provider definitions, provider-specific binaries, scripts, arbitrary plugins, or configuration-loaded code.\n- Making `AProvider` a workflow, lifecycle, or authorization surface.\n- Provider lifecycle redesign already owned by #855.\n- Provider failure/recovery qualification owned by #901.\n- Model downloads, paid-provider calls, benchmark claims, or hardware provisioning.\n- Folding #900 qualification evidence into this implementation issue.\n\nFollow-up to #514, #876, and #855. Discovered during #900. Coordinate with the planned provider redesign so this remains its bounded inference-configuration slice.\n","title":"[provider architecture] Make declared AProvider inference configuration effective and observable"},"operator_approval":null,"pull_request":null,"recovery":null,"repository":"agent-logic/agent-design-language"},"schema":"csdlc.v3.staged_github_mutation.v1"},"native":{"identity":"86238ac773496ecb795f5b72fd8e85c571293c865b2bd1fc77efdbfa4f918774","owner":"csdlc-v3-github"},"origin":{"kind":"prepared","source":{"checkout":"/Users/daniel/git/agent-design-language","common":"/Users/daniel/git/agent-design-language/.git","head":"9c18d682bf28f88db8b47279ac2ab5ab2e41db68","repository":"agent-logic/agent-design-language"}}}